## 1. LaTeX for SI Section S3

```latex
% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
\section*{SI Section S3: Alignment and the Cost of a Redundant Coefficient}
\label{sec:si_alignment}

\noindent This section derives the two results used in the main text: the
alignment condition behind the null $\beta_{\mathrm{A}}+\beta_{\mathrm{B}}=0$
(\S2.1--2.2), and the finite-sample cost $1/(2n)$ of fitting the pair when the
difference suffices (\S2.4).  Both are classical; we state them in the notation
of this paper.

\subsection*{S3.1 Alignment as a linear restriction}

Let $Y\in\{0,1\}$ indicate a home win, and let $A$ and $B$ be the home and away
values of one KPI.  The pair model is
\begin{equation}
  \operatorname{logit}\Pr(Y=1\mid A,B)=\beta_{0}+\beta_{\mathrm{A}}A+\beta_{\mathrm{B}}B .
  \label{eq:si_pair}
\end{equation}
Writing $D=A-B$ and $S=A+B$, so that $A=(S+D)/2$ and $B=(S-D)/2$, the linear
score becomes
\begin{equation}
  \beta_{\mathrm{A}}A+\beta_{\mathrm{B}}B
  =\tfrac12(\beta_{\mathrm{A}}-\beta_{\mathrm{B}})\,D
  +\tfrac12(\beta_{\mathrm{A}}+\beta_{\mathrm{B}})\,S .
  \label{eq:si_rotation}
\end{equation}
The difference model $\operatorname{logit}\Pr(Y=1\mid D)=\gamma_{0}+\gamma D$ is
therefore the pair model with the single linear restriction
$\beta_{\mathrm{A}}+\beta_{\mathrm{B}}=0$, with $\gamma=\beta_{\mathrm{A}}=-\beta_{\mathrm{B}}$.
When the restriction holds, $\Pr(Y=1\mid A,B)$ depends on $(A,B)$ only through
$D$; within the model, $D$ is sufficient for the pair and nothing is lost by
differencing.  We call the KPI \emph{aligned} in this case.  If the logistic
model is misspecified, the same statements apply to the pseudo-true parameters
that minimise expected log-loss \parencite{white1982}.

The restriction is invariant to centring and to rescaling $A$ and $B$ by a
\emph{common} factor, which is why both series are scaled by their pooled
standard deviation (\S3.4).  Rescaling them by \emph{separate} factors would
change the null hypothesis.

Because the two models are nested and differ by one restriction, the
likelihood-ratio statistic $\Lambda=2(\ell_{(\mathrm{A},\mathrm{B})}-\ell_{D})$
is asymptotically $\chi^{2}_{1}$ under the null for independent observations
\parencite{wilks1938}.  Under local misalignment it is approximately noncentral
$\chi^{2}_{1}$ with noncentrality $\lambda$, so that
$\mathbb{E}[\Lambda]\approx 1+\lambda$.  Team recurrence makes matches
dependent, so the $\chi^{2}_{1}$ reference is a landmark here rather than a
calibrated null.

\subsection*{S3.2 Expected excess test log-loss of a fitted model}

Let $\ell(\theta;x,y)$ be the log-likelihood of one observation under a regular
parametric model with $k$ free parameters, and let
$R(\theta)=-\mathbb{E}\,\ell(\theta;X,Y)$ be its expected log-loss on a new
observation.  Let $\theta^{*}$ minimise $R$, and let $\hat\theta$ be the
maximum-likelihood estimate from $n$ independent training observations.  A
second-order expansion of $R$ about $\theta^{*}$ gives
\begin{equation}
  R(\hat\theta)\approx R(\theta^{*})
  +\tfrac12(\hat\theta-\theta^{*})^{\top}J\,(\hat\theta-\theta^{*}),
  \qquad J=\mathbb{E}\bigl[-\nabla^{2}\ell(\theta^{*};X,Y)\bigr].
\end{equation}
Since $\sqrt{n}(\hat\theta-\theta^{*})\to\mathcal{N}(0,J^{-1}KJ^{-1})$ with
$K=\mathbb{E}\bigl[\nabla\ell\,\nabla\ell^{\top}\bigr]$ \parencite{white1982},
\begin{equation}
  \mathbb{E}\bigl[R(\hat\theta)\bigr]-R(\theta^{*})
  \approx\frac{\operatorname{tr}(J^{-1}K)}{2n}.
  \label{eq:si_excess}
\end{equation}
When the model is correctly specified, $K=J$ and the excess is $k/(2n)$.  This
is the per-parameter penalty underlying Akaike's information criterion
\parencite{akaike1973}; \cref{eq:si_excess} is the general form behind
Takeuchi's criterion \parencite{konishi2008}.  No distributional assumption on
the features is required beyond regularity.

\subsection*{S3.3 The cost of fitting the pair when the difference suffices}

Suppose the KPI is aligned.  The pseudo-true pair parameter then satisfies the
restriction, so the pair model (three parameters) and the difference model (two
parameters) attain the same population risk $R(\theta^{*})$.  Applying
\cref{eq:si_excess} to each model with correct specification,
\begin{equation}
  \mathbb{E}\bigl[\mathrm{LL}(D)-\mathrm{LL}(A,B)\bigr]
  \approx\frac{2}{2n}-\frac{3}{2n}=-\frac{1}{2n}.
  \label{eq:si_cost}
\end{equation}
Fitting the pair spends one redundant coefficient, and the difference is
expected to have lower test log-loss by about $1/(2n)$ nats per match.  Under
cross-validation, $n$ is the size of each training set, not the full sample.

If the KPI is not aligned, let $\Delta^{*}>0$ be the population log-loss lost by
imposing the restriction.  The expected gap becomes
\begin{equation}
  \mathbb{E}\bigl[\mathrm{LL}(D)-\mathrm{LL}(A,B)\bigr]
  \approx\Delta^{*}-\frac{1}{2n},
  \label{eq:si_crossover}
\end{equation}
so the difference is still expected to predict better whenever the
misalignment costs less than the redundant coefficient.  Under local
alternatives the noncentrality of $\Lambda$ is $\lambda\approx2n\Delta^{*}$, so
the crossover $\Delta^{*}=1/(2n)$ corresponds to $\lambda=1$, that is to
$\mathbb{E}[\Lambda]\approx2$.  This is Akaike's threshold for choosing between
nested models that differ by one parameter, and it matches the asymptotic
equivalence between Akaike's criterion and cross-validation
\parencite{stone1977}.  For out-of-sample feature choice, $\Lambda=2$ is
therefore the natural landmark; the $\chi^{2}_{1}$ 5\% point $3.84$ answers a
different question (rejecting exact alignment).

\subsection*{S3.4 Several KPIs}

The same argument applies to a model with $p$ KPIs.  Entering every KPI as a
pair rather than as a difference adds $p$ coefficients, so when all are aligned
the expected cost is approximately $p/(2n)$.  Redundancy that is negligible for
one KPI can therefore become material in multi-KPI models.

\subsection*{S3.5 Scope}

The expansions are first-order in $1/n$ and assume independent observations
and a regular model.  Team recurrence and leave-one-team-out folds make matches
dependent and folds unequal, so \cref{eq:si_cost,eq:si_crossover} are
approximations for these leagues rather than exact predictions.  Under
misspecification, $1/(2n)$ in \cref{eq:si_cost} is replaced by the
difference in the two models' values of $\operatorname{tr}(J^{-1}K)/(2n)$.
```

