## 3. What this changes in the main text

**§2.2 and Figure 1A: add 2 as the primary landmark.** Section S3.3 shows that Λ ≈ 2 marks the crossover where the difference stops being expected to beat the pair out of sample. I'd draw lines at both 2 and 3.84 in Figure 1A, labelled "AIC crossover" and "χ²₁ 5% point". The text would then say that 2 is the relevant reference for feature choice, while 3.84 concerns rejecting exact alignment.

This also makes your results more interesting. Your medians (0.67 and 0.71) sit well below 2, so for most KPIs the difference is expected to predict at least as well as the pair. The KPIs between 2 and 3.84 are the ones where the pair is expected to win out of sample even though a significance test wouldn't flag them. Counting those per sport would be informative.

**§2.4: remove "under the companion's Gaussian designs".** The 1/(2n) cost needs only a correctly specified regular model (S3.2), not Gaussian features. Replace the sentence with a pointer to SI S3 and the classical citations. That removes the main dependence on the unpublished companion.

**§4.2 and §5.1: frame the comparison as a prediction.** Section S3.3 gives E[LL(D) − LL(A, B)] ≈ −1/(2n_train) under alignment. Report it against the fold-size-based values (about 2.0 milli-nats for rugby and 0.49 for football, to be recomputed from the actual folds), with the bootstrap intervals for the medians.

**§2.3 and §5.4: trim the references to the companion.** With S3 in place, "proofs of alignment … belong to the statistics companion" can become "derivations are given in SI Section S3". The undefined terms ("relative-to-single-entity ratio under exchangeability", "stress boundary") can then go.

**The multivariable analysis gains theoretical backing.** Section S3.4 is the justification for the p/(2n) argument in item B3 of the review, so it supports that addition directly.

One limitation the SI states, which a careful referee would also raise: under team recurrence and leave-one-team-out, the iid expansions are approximate. That is why S3.5 calls the results approximations for these leagues. A simple robustness check would be to estimate tr(J⁻¹K) with a team-clustered K for a few KPIs and confirm it stays close to the parameter count.